(5136105, 745417, 66384, '<td valign="top">Well I really like this, but then I''m weird. :P It looks like an illustration of Excalibur. <br/> <br/>It''s  creative, has lovely color, and a very pleasing composition. I might have taken off a little of the bottom for balance.<br/> <br/>I had the same problem with the sunset -- not well situated here for a good sunset photo. We have beautiful sunsets, but I have to go a long way to get a good view. There are power lines everywhere. It''s so maddening.</td>', 'Well I really like this, but then I''m weird. :P It looks like an illustration of Excalibur.  It''s  creative, has lovely color, and a very pleasing composition. I might have taken off a little of the bottom for balance. I had the same problem with the sunset -- not well situated here for a good sunset photo. We have beautiful sunsets, but I have to go a long way to get a good view. There are power lines everywhere. It''s so maddening.', '2008-12-17 01:06:56.000000', NULL, 0),
(5136224, 745485, 31727, '<td valign="top">Very nice it is a one photograph that will live on as a moment,Harvey Sherman</td>', 'Very nice it is a one photograph that will live on as a moment,Harvey Sherman', '2008-12-17 02:25:41.000000', NULL, 0),
(5136327, 745474, 62357, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/>The first thing I notice with this picture is the wonderful reds from the sunset.  Absolutely gorgeous.  However, as somebody once commented on one of my photos, you do also need to have a subject, it is not enough just to photograph the sky.  In a challenge with so many wonderful sunset/sunrise pics, you would really need to stand out by having an interesting subject and focal point.  Here there really is nothing.  <br/> <br/>From a technical point of view the exposure is good, as are the colours.  I am not sure I agree with the sharpening comments, I think the clouds look good as they are.  Focus looks quite soft.<br/> <br/>I think the main reason you did not score very highly in this challenge, is the lack of a real subject.  <br/> <br/>Hope you find this useful.<br/> <br/>Sarah</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club  The first thing I notice with this picture is the wonderful reds from the sunset.  Absolutely gorgeous.  However, as somebody once commented on one of my photos, you do also need to have a subject, it is not enough just to photograph the sky.  In a challenge with so many wonderful sunset/sunrise pics, you would really need to stand out by having an interesting subject and focal point.  Here there really is nothing.   From a technical point of view the exposure is good, as are the colours.  I am not sure I agree with the sharpening comments, I think the clouds look good as they are.  Focus looks quite soft. I think the main reason you did not score very highly in this challenge, is the lack of a real subject.   Hope you find this useful. Sarah', '2008-12-17 03:36:36.000000', NULL, 0),
(5136377, 744144, 28742, '<td valign="top">that''s cool! ;-)</td>', 'that''s cool! ;-)', '2008-12-17 04:45:44.000000', NULL, 0),
(5136454, 745204, 84932, '<td valign="top">Gorgeous,it evokes so many emotions.</td>', 'Gorgeous,it evokes so many emotions.', '2008-12-17 07:01:11.000000', NULL, 0),
(5136482, 745205, 99497, '<td valign="top">In my opinion a very artistic photograph. Personally I will rate it much higher than all the ribbon winners for this challenge</td>', 'In my opinion a very artistic photograph. Personally I will rate it much higher than all the ribbon winners for this challenge', '2008-12-17 07:38:10.000000', NULL, 0),
(5136497, 745570, 62357, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/>So, I get to critique a Posthumous image - good job these are randomly generated.  Don''t shoot me :)<br/> <br/>I am pretty sure you are aware of the reasons why you did not score higher, even without reading others comments or mine.  I actually scored this a 6 because I loved the light captured on the trees and it was refreshing to see something other than a landscape for the subject.  I do find the area just behind the cow to be a little too dark for my taste, but difficult lighting at that time of day.<br/> <br/>I could add to others comments that the positioning of the cow was too central, or that the top of the tree was chopped off etc, etc, but really I believe those comments would be superfluous.  It is a pity the trees are slightly out of focus too.<br/> <br/>I haven''t figured out if you were genuintely going for the brown or not. <br/> <br/>Sorry I have not added any substance to this critique, but you know, it''s pretty challenging to to critique this one!<br/>Sarah<br/> <br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club  So, I get to critique a Posthumous image - good job these are randomly generated.  Don''t shoot me :) I am pretty sure you are aware of the reasons why you did not score higher, even without reading others comments or mine.  I actually scored this a 6 because I loved the light captured on the trees and it was refreshing to see something other than a landscape for the subject.  I do find the area just behind the cow to be a little too dark for my taste, but difficult lighting at that time of day. I could add to others comments that the positioning of the cow was too central, or that the top of the tree was chopped off etc, etc, but really I believe those comments would be superfluous.  It is a pity the trees are slightly out of focus too. I haven''t figured out if you were genuintely going for the brown or not.  Sorry I have not added any substance to this critique, but you know, it''s pretty challenging to to critique this one!Sarah ', '2008-12-17 07:49:30.000000', NULL, 0);
INSERT INTO comments ("id", "image_id", "commenter_id", "raw_comment", "comment", "date", "edited", "made_during_challenge") VALUES
(5136643, 745568, 9932, '<td valign="top">Danny (crabappl3)!<br/> <br/>How have you been!<br/>I have not been able to be around much on DPC but good to see you back!<br/> <br/>Rob<br/></td>', 'Danny (crabappl3)! How have you been!I have not been able to be around much on DPC but good to see you back! Rob', '2008-12-17 08:56:37.000000', NULL, 0),
(5137408, 745616, 62357, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/>First of all, let me say, I did like your title.  I thought it was quite fitting for this challenge.<br/> <br/>The concept is very nice, the dying flowers silhouetted against the sky.  However, there are a few things that may have contributed to you lowish score.  Hopefully, my comments will help you.  <br/> <br/>It looks very much like the horizon is actually a few degrees away from being level.  You can see this if you look at the water, it has an appearance of tilting to the left.  A quick rotation adjustment in Photoshop would have helped this.   The fence posts in the foreground of the picture do not really add anything to the composition and make it feel quite cluttered.  Another potential reason for the score is that voters possibly felt it didn''t really portray a sunset (some people literally want to see the sun!).<br/> <br/>Overall a good image that probably could have been improved a bit with a little bit of editing.<br/> <br/>Sarah</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club  First of all, let me say, I did like your title.  I thought it was quite fitting for this challenge. The concept is very nice, the dying flowers silhouetted against the sky.  However, there are a few things that may have contributed to you lowish score.  Hopefully, my comments will help you.   It looks very much like the horizon is actually a few degrees away from being level.  You can see this if you look at the water, it has an appearance of tilting to the left.  A quick rotation adjustment in Photoshop would have helped this.   The fence posts in the foreground of the picture do not really add anything to the composition and make it feel quite cluttered.  Another potential reason for the score is that voters possibly felt it didn''t really portray a sunset (some people literally want to see the sun!). Overall a good image that probably could have been improved a bit with a little bit of editing. Sarah', '2008-12-17 14:01:08.000000', NULL, 0),
(5137469, 745439, 61651, '<td valign="top">Very moody - I think this gets across an emotive aspect that a lot of sharper entries lack.</td>', 'Very moody - I think this gets across an emotive aspect that a lot of sharper entries lack.', '2008-12-17 14:39:11.000000', NULL, 0),
(5137546, 745603, 30982, '<td valign="top">The light in the window seals the deal on this one.</td>', 'The light in the window seals the deal on this one.', '2008-12-17 15:34:19.000000', NULL, 0),
(5137681, 745417, 70514, '<td valign="top">Great idea though.  I like the shining light hitting off the corner.  (What a tough challenge)</td>', 'Great idea though.  I like the shining light hitting off the corner.  (What a tough challenge)', '2008-12-17 16:41:59.000000', NULL, 0),
(5137848, 745204, 3037, '<td valign="top">Wonderful as always!</td>', 'Wonderful as always!', '2008-12-17 18:17:38.000000', NULL, 0),
(5137916, 744821, 35638, '<td valign="top">Was this taken from the Beeline?  <br/>Beautiful picture.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2008-12-17 19:04:35.</i></td>', 'Was this taken from the Beeline?  Beautiful picture.', '2008-12-17 19:03:14.000000', '2008-12-17 19:04:35.000000', 0),
(5138360, 745585, 58785, '<td valign="top">Hello from the Critique Club ;)<br/> <br/>Some of DPC voters must be drunk when they vote. 1s? man, why don''t you kick your dog instead of giving 1s to people when your partner refuse to sleep with you that night? Dang.<br/> <br/>Anyway, let''s get down to business.<br/> <br/>Technically very nice. A little bit burnt out but not much. Nice colors on top, blue and orange-ish colors very nice. Contrast is nice. Composition is also good here. I am keep checking the score, 5.2? Wow, pretty low for this shot. I would give around 7 if I did vote, but didn''t.<br/> <br/>I looked at your photos on your page. Man, you have a good future. Keep on clicking. You do have the "photographer''s eye". Nice compositions and ideas.<br/> <br/>I just don''t believe you don''t have any 6+ yet, but very soon... it seems like you will get one very very soon :)<br/> <br/>Good luck on your next one :)<br/> <br/>Leo</td>', 'Hello from the Critique Club ;) Some of DPC voters must be drunk when they vote. 1s? man, why don''t you kick your dog instead of giving 1s to people when your partner refuse to sleep with you that night? Dang. Anyway, let''s get down to business. Technically very nice. A little bit burnt out but not much. Nice colors on top, blue and orange-ish colors very nice. Contrast is nice. Composition is also good here. I am keep checking the score, 5.2? Wow, pretty low for this shot. I would give around 7 if I did vote, but didn''t. I looked at your photos on your page. Man, you have a good future. Keep on clicking. You do have the "photographer''s eye". Nice compositions and ideas. I just don''t believe you don''t have any 6+ yet, but very soon... it seems like you will get one very very soon :) Good luck on your next one :) Leo', '2008-12-18 00:17:01.000000', NULL, 0),
(5138369, 745391, 58785, '<td valign="top">Hello from the Critique Club ;)<br/> <br/>Ah... nice but not quite perfect. I sense what you see here, but things are not lined up correctly and I am not talking about the ducks ;)<br/> <br/>Ok, why did you get low scores, it might be because the ducks are not passing in order? the dirt in the water? the view a bit far?<br/> <br/>all above?<br/> <br/>Ok, technically not bad. Nicely exposed sharp photo. Focus seems like where it should be. But the composition here little off, I think you could go down a little more, give yourself a little tight angle and shoot bit more zoomed in, or cropped to give dramatic lights a bit more pop.<br/> <br/>Oh well, try more options next time, and you should take more photos of this scene... very nice place :)<br/> <br/>Checked your page and "Waiting for the chaos to begin..." is a very nice shot. I love it.<br/> <br/>My friend, you do have future. Keep on clicking ;)<br/> <br/>Leo</td>', 'Hello from the Critique Club ;) Ah... nice but not quite perfect. I sense what you see here, but things are not lined up correctly and I am not talking about the ducks ;) Ok, why did you get low scores, it might be because the ducks are not passing in order? the dirt in the water? the view a bit far? all above? Ok, technically not bad. Nicely exposed sharp photo. Focus seems like where it should be. But the composition here little off, I think you could go down a little more, give yourself a little tight angle and shoot bit more zoomed in, or cropped to give dramatic lights a bit more pop. Oh well, try more options next time, and you should take more photos of this scene... very nice place :) Checked your page and "Waiting for the chaos to begin..." is a very nice shot. I love it. My friend, you do have future. Keep on clicking ;) Leo', '2008-12-18 00:25:17.000000', NULL, 0),
(5138401, 745448, 58785, '<td valign="top">Hello from the Critique Club ;)<br/> <br/>Your composition on other photos are very very good. I envy you to have such good eye, and actually trying similar styles myself :)<br/> <br/>Your "Power Station and the Gas Works" is a little weak photo for your standards it seems. I like only one thing on that photo, colors. don''t like the smoke, and the crop too much. Technically very nicely exposed photo. There is too much dark area on the bottom for my taste. Image stays small because of the way it cropped.<br/> <br/>I know you can do much better work than this one. 5.2 might be around what this photo deserves, I would give 4 or 5 if I did vote, but didn''t.<br/> <br/>Good luck on your next, and keep up the god work (Not like this one though)<br/> <br/>Leo</td>', 'Hello from the Critique Club ;) Your composition on other photos are very very good. I envy you to have such good eye, and actually trying similar styles myself :) Your "Power Station and the Gas Works" is a little weak photo for your standards it seems. I like only one thing on that photo, colors. don''t like the smoke, and the crop too much. Technically very nicely exposed photo. There is too much dark area on the bottom for my taste. Image stays small because of the way it cropped. I know you can do much better work than this one. 5.2 might be around what this photo deserves, I would give 4 or 5 if I did vote, but didn''t. Good luck on your next, and keep up the god work (Not like this one though) Leo', '2008-12-18 01:04:03.000000', NULL, 0),
(5138533, 745204, 83535, '<td valign="top">Very cool!  I live here and have not done this angle!  Sometimes it is not easy to get around those rangers.  They want everyone out exactly at sunset, and as you probably know, sometimes right after sunset is the best time to take some shots.  Good job on the early morning thought!</td>', 'Very cool!  I live here and have not done this angle!  Sometimes it is not easy to get around those rangers.  They want everyone out exactly at sunset, and as you probably know, sometimes right after sunset is the best time to take some shots.  Good job on the early morning thought!', '2008-12-18 03:51:07.000000', NULL, 0),
(5138665, 744605, 29156, '<td valign="top">the best</td>', 'the best', '2008-12-18 07:42:16.000000', NULL, 0),
(5138666, 744144, 29156, '<td valign="top">beautiful, should score higher</td>', 'beautiful, should score higher', '2008-12-18 07:43:44.000000', NULL, 0),
(5138769, 744787, 96664, '<td valign="top">IMO I think a higher score was needed here... Great shot!</td>', 'IMO I think a higher score was needed here... Great shot!', '2008-12-18 09:14:13.000000', NULL, 0),
(5138947, 745580, 67237, '<td valign="top">I think you made a a very good work with the editing on your small laptop.<br/>My only nitpick is the level of vignetting (whether natural or pp) on upper right and lower left corners.<br/></td>', 'I think you made a a very good work with the editing on your small laptop.My only nitpick is the level of vignetting (whether natural or pp) on upper right and lower left corners.', '2008-12-18 10:46:29.000000', NULL, 0),
(5139108, 745204, 56744, '<td valign="top">That''s the way I like it... Records are made to be broken! Congrats Jason on so many things: - A stunning image, a great score, a personal best and a blue ribbon. A great companion to your Solitude''s Dawn. You out did yourself with this one, and Im loving your work.<br/></td>', 'That''s the way I like it... Records are made to be broken! Congrats Jason on so many things: - A stunning image, a great score, a personal best and a blue ribbon. A great companion to your Solitude''s Dawn. You out did yourself with this one, and Im loving your work.', '2008-12-18 12:12:08.000000', NULL, 0),
(5139201, 745204, 101736, '<td valign="top">hey Congrats,<br/>I am a first-time user and very impressed with your image.  What''s the spec? camera, lens, etc....<br/>Cheers~~</td>', 'hey Congrats,I am a first-time user and very impressed with your image.  What''s the spec? camera, lens, etc....Cheers~~', '2008-12-18 13:07:48.000000', NULL, 0),
(5139821, 745437, 58785, '<td valign="top">Hello from critique club :)<br/> <br/>Technically little dark photo, focused nicely but having boats on the middle of the picture was not a good idea. After all, you are trying to show us the sunset. The sky colors on the background nice but almost lost in whole photo. Try to look at this photo with tighter crop, say put the boats on left bottom somewhere and show us more of that wonderful sky. Little more brightness, you could be around 5.6 to 6. You might need a bit more sharpening though if you cropped that much.<br/> <br/>Second entry and doing well. Points are going right direction. Check the previous winner photos in DPC. Don''t duplicate them but they are good samples of good work, you should learn something from them.<br/> <br/>My advice, keep it clean, simple and sharp.<br/> <br/>Good luck on your next<br/> <br/>Leo</td>', 'Hello from critique club :) Technically little dark photo, focused nicely but having boats on the middle of the picture was not a good idea. After all, you are trying to show us the sunset. The sky colors on the background nice but almost lost in whole photo. Try to look at this photo with tighter crop, say put the boats on left bottom somewhere and show us more of that wonderful sky. Little more brightness, you could be around 5.6 to 6. You might need a bit more sharpening though if you cropped that much. Second entry and doing well. Points are going right direction. Check the previous winner photos in DPC. Don''t duplicate them but they are good samples of good work, you should learn something from them. My advice, keep it clean, simple and sharp. Good luck on your next Leo', '2008-12-18 20:23:28.000000', NULL, 0),
(5140247, 745182, 62357, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club<br/> <br/>I think the score you received is probably around about right for this shot.  This is indeed a beautiful sunrise, however, there are a number of things that could have improved the score quite easily. <br/> <br/>The extreme shadows in the foreground do actually hurt this one a little bit.  If the foreground was slightly lighter the viewer would be able to put the excellent leading line more into context.  Right now it looks a little out of place.  <br/> <br/>Composition: if you had perhaps taken a few steps to the right so the subject was more to the left of the frame you could have made better use of the leading line.  Also, to my eye the horizon looks like it is right in the middle of the photo. I think including a little bit more of the sky may have helped this.<br/> <br/>Overall a very nice sunrise photo that potentially could have scored a little higher with some additional editing.<br/> <br/>Sarah</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club I think the score you received is probably around about right for this shot.  This is indeed a beautiful sunrise, however, there are a number of things that could have improved the score quite easily.  The extreme shadows in the foreground do actually hurt this one a little bit.  If the foreground was slightly lighter the viewer would be able to put the excellent leading line more into context.  Right now it looks a little out of place.   Composition: if you had perhaps taken a few steps to the right so the subject was more to the left of the frame you could have made better use of the leading line.  Also, to my eye the horizon looks like it is right in the middle of the photo. I think including a little bit more of the sky may have helped this. Overall a very nice sunrise photo that potentially could have scored a little higher with some additional editing. Sarah', '2008-12-19 04:28:41.000000', NULL, 0),
(5140248, 745571, 62357, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club<br/> <br/>First of all many congratulations on 6th place.  Well deserved, this is a lovely image.<br/> <br/>Well, it is, I must say, rather difficult to provide tips on how to improve this photo.  Composition is excellent, technically I can''t see much wrong with it. The colours are very subtle.  The only thing I really am not that fond of is the heavy border.  I am not suggesting a border is bad, but these feels just a little bit heavy for such a delicately coloured image.  I think if you had a smaller border you may have even ribboned!<br/> <br/>I did actually give this a 7 and probably would have given an 8 if the border was more subtle.<br/> <br/>Sarah</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club First of all many congratulations on 6th place.  Well deserved, this is a lovely image. Well, it is, I must say, rather difficult to provide tips on how to improve this photo.  Composition is excellent, technically I can''t see much wrong with it. The colours are very subtle.  The only thing I really am not that fond of is the heavy border.  I am not suggesting a border is bad, but these feels just a little bit heavy for such a delicately coloured image.  I think if you had a smaller border you may have even ribboned! I did actually give this a 7 and probably would have given an 8 if the border was more subtle. Sarah', '2008-12-19 04:34:22.000000', NULL, 0),
(5140264, 745296, 62357, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critigue Club<br/> <br/>I''m sorry this didn''t score better for you, but I think there are a number of things contributing to the low score, mostly relating to the processing.<br/> <br/>The composition itself is good and you captured the sky rather nicely.  However, as the image has rather a lot of noise in it, which surprises me given the caliber of your equipment, it suggests that you have cropped this image rather heavily during post processing.   The image itself does look a little as though it could be straightened, although that is possibly just an illusion due to the angle.  The focus also looks a little soft, again, I wonder if that is a product of using a zoom/heavy cropping.<br/> <br/>As this was an advanced editing challenge you could probably have cloned out the suspension wire that is visible.  It think this may have helped, although, of course, it would be quite a big job.  <br/> <br/>I think most of your comments received should have given you some pointers on how to improve a shot like this for the future.<br/> <br/>Sarah</td>', 'Greetings from the Critigue Club I''m sorry this didn''t score better for you, but I think there are a number of things contributing to the low score, mostly relating to the processing. The composition itself is good and you captured the sky rather nicely.  However, as the image has rather a lot of noise in it, which surprises me given the caliber of your equipment, it suggests that you have cropped this image rather heavily during post processing.   The image itself does look a little as though it could be straightened, although that is possibly just an illusion due to the angle.  The focus also looks a little soft, again, I wonder if that is a product of using a zoom/heavy cropping. As this was an advanced editing challenge you could probably have cloned out the suspension wire that is visible.  It think this may have helped, although, of course, it would be quite a big job.   I think most of your comments received should have given you some pointers on how to improve a shot like this for the future. Sarah', '2008-12-19 05:59:04.000000', NULL, 0),
(5140290, 745355, 62357, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club<br/> <br/>I was surprised to see this scored only a 5.1, I think it probably was worth a higher score than that.  I have read the comments left during voting and can see that people did not like the artificial lighting you used.  When viewing this again, after the challenge, I am inclined to agree with those comments.  I actually gave you a six, as I did really like the composition.  However, I do agree the person in the shot looks somewhat isolated due to the lighting.<br/> <br/>The composition is strong, but the focus looks a little soft.  The trees and hill in the background look quite soft.  I have to agree with Aliqui''s comment about the vignette.  I do not think this was probably necessary for this particular photo.  I think it detracts from the overall composition.<br/> <br/>The colour in the sky looks a little bit unnatural and I think this may have hurt you a bit.<br/> <br/>Overall I think this is a nice sunset photo, but given the challenge it was in, certainly does not have anywhere near enough WOW to woo the DPC voters.<br/> <br/>Sarah</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club I was surprised to see this scored only a 5.1, I think it probably was worth a higher score than that.  I have read the comments left during voting and can see that people did not like the artificial lighting you used.  When viewing this again, after the challenge, I am inclined to agree with those comments.  I actually gave you a six, as I did really like the composition.  However, I do agree the person in the shot looks somewhat isolated due to the lighting. The composition is strong, but the focus looks a little soft.  The trees and hill in the background look quite soft.  I have to agree with Aliqui''s comment about the vignette.  I do not think this was probably necessary for this particular photo.  I think it detracts from the overall composition. The colour in the sky looks a little bit unnatural and I think this may have hurt you a bit. Overall I think this is a nice sunset photo, but given the challenge it was in, certainly does not have anywhere near enough WOW to woo the DPC voters. Sarah', '2008-12-19 06:48:25.000000', NULL, 0),
(5140293, 745338, 62357, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club<br/> <br/>I think the sunset must have been amazing - would love to have seen it myself.<br/> <br/>As far as the composition goes, I think this would have been much better in a horizontal orientation.  The mountain to the right is cropped right through the middle and my eye is drawn immediately to that.  You''ve done a great job with Photomatix as you have managed not to increase the noise levels too much (always hard with low contrast scenes) and have avoided the "cartoon" look.  The reds are amazing - not sure how pumped they were in editing, but either way I feel like I can imagine the sunset.<br/> <br/>A couple of other reasons you may have scored a bit lower than perhaps you would have hoped: in a sunset/sunrise challenge some people may actually be looking to see the sun itself in the frame.  Also, in a challenge with such strong competition I think this probably lacks the WOW factor, but largely, I think the main reason is the orientation of the photo.<br/> <br/>Hope some of this was helpful.<br/> <br/>Sarah<br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club I think the sunset must have been amazing - would love to have seen it myself. As far as the composition goes, I think this would have been much better in a horizontal orientation.  The mountain to the right is cropped right through the middle and my eye is drawn immediately to that.  You''ve done a great job with Photomatix as you have managed not to increase the noise levels too much (always hard with low contrast scenes) and have avoided the "cartoon" look.  The reds are amazing - not sure how pumped they were in editing, but either way I feel like I can imagine the sunset. A couple of other reasons you may have scored a bit lower than perhaps you would have hoped: in a sunset/sunrise challenge some people may actually be looking to see the sun itself in the frame.  Also, in a challenge with such strong competition I think this probably lacks the WOW factor, but largely, I think the main reason is the orientation of the photo. Hope some of this was helpful. Sarah', '2008-12-19 06:59:58.000000', NULL, 0),
(5140304, 745482, 62357, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club<br/> <br/>I rather liked this during voting (gave it a 6) and was surprised it didn''t score a little higher.  <br/> <br/>I think the commenters have pretty much nailed most of the reasons why it didn''t score higher.  There is possibly too much reliance on the title, as the elderly man is really rather lost in this.  The composition is good the way it is, but in order to emphasise the subject (as your title has made the man the subject), it would probably have been better to crop a little tighter.  The tracks on the ice are great, but I am inclined to agree with <img alt="'' . substr(''//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21_F.gif'', strrpos(''//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21_F.gif'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21_F.gif"/> <a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/profile.php?USER_ID=68504" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">tnun</a> about cropping out the stones.<br/> <br/>I think I would also probably suggest a slight levels adjustment to lighten the midtones.  You could get a bit  more detail out of the trees which I think would have helped.  Also, as this was a sunset/sunrise challenge, I wonder if a slight boost to the red/yellow saturation would have helped your score.  It''s such a lovely wintery scene, I just don''t think it has reached it''s potential.  It may be a tad too sharp as well.<br/> <br/>Hope this was helfpul. Give it a go with a slight crop and levels adjustment. I''d be interested to see how it came out.<br/> <br/>Sarah</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club I rather liked this during voting (gave it a 6) and was surprised it didn''t score a little higher.   I think the commenters have pretty much nailed most of the reasons why it didn''t score higher.  There is possibly too much reliance on the title, as the elderly man is really rather lost in this.  The composition is good the way it is, but in order to emphasise the subject (as your title has made the man the subject), it would probably have been better to crop a little tighter.  The tracks on the ice are great, but I am inclined to agree with  tnun about cropping out the stones. I think I would also probably suggest a slight levels adjustment to lighten the midtones.  You could get a bit  more detail out of the trees which I think would have helped.  Also, as this was a sunset/sunrise challenge, I wonder if a slight boost to the red/yellow saturation would have helped your score.  It''s such a lovely wintery scene, I just don''t think it has reached it''s potential.  It may be a tad too sharp as well. Hope this was helfpul. Give it a go with a slight crop and levels adjustment. I''d be interested to see how it came out. Sarah', '2008-12-19 07:21:55.000000', NULL, 0),
(5140367, 745204, 18655, '<td valign="top">Congrats Jason! Very well deserved blue. Georgeus shot!</td>', 'Congrats Jason! Very well deserved blue. Georgeus shot!', '2008-12-19 07:57:19.000000', NULL, 0),
(5140457, 744201, 36065, '<td valign="top"><b>Hey there from the Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/><b>Camera Work/Technical:</b>  Great job stopping the action in the shot.  That 1/4000 shutter speed did this one justice.  I also like the low noise you managed, even with an ISO of 640.  Your background coloring is also very nice, and is one of strengths of the image.<br/> <br/><b>Lighting:</b> The backlighting you managed here is spectacular.  I like the glow you caught in the wings, as well as the nicely silhouetted figure of the bird.<br/> <br/><b>Composition/Content:</b> I think that I will agree with Pedro in that the image would have been even stronger with some of the negative space and color graduation cropped from the bottom.  Perhaps a square crop would have worked better than the light that falls into darkness at the bottom.<br/> <br/><b>My Opinion:</b> 6.27 is a very strong score, and I think that it was an appropriate score for this image.  Looking through the challenge entries for this one, I believe that your entry placed appropriately.<br/> <br/>Thank you for the opportunity to provide a critique on your entry,<br/><b>Eric</b> <br/></td>', 'Hey there from the Critique Club  Camera Work/Technical:  Great job stopping the action in the shot.  That 1/4000 shutter speed did this one justice.  I also like the low noise you managed, even with an ISO of 640.  Your background coloring is also very nice, and is one of strengths of the image. Lighting: The backlighting you managed here is spectacular.  I like the glow you caught in the wings, as well as the nicely silhouetted figure of the bird. Composition/Content: I think that I will agree with Pedro in that the image would have been even stronger with some of the negative space and color graduation cropped from the bottom.  Perhaps a square crop would have worked better than the light that falls into darkness at the bottom. My Opinion: 6.27 is a very strong score, and I think that it was an appropriate score for this image.  Looking through the challenge entries for this one, I believe that your entry placed appropriately. Thank you for the opportunity to provide a critique on your entry,Eric ', '2008-12-19 08:41:33.000000', NULL, 0),
(5140493, 745199, 36065, '<td valign="top"><b>Hey there from the Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/><b>Camera Work/Technical:</b> I tend to agree with Derek here.  The saturation, contrast, and the softness of the clouds make my eye immediately disagree with what its seeing.  Unfortunately, that is one of the losses of HDR.<br/> <br/><b>Lighting:</b> I like the color layer you have at the bottom of the frame.  I''d really like to see this one processed without the HDR.<br/> <br/><b>Composition/Content:</b> Nicely composed, and a perfectly straight horizon.  Very nice.<br/> <br/><b>My Opinion:</b> I think that this entry was scored and placed appropriately.<br/> <br/>Thank you for the opportunity to provide a critique on your entry,<br/><b>Eric</b> <br/></td>', 'Hey there from the Critique Club  Camera Work/Technical: I tend to agree with Derek here.  The saturation, contrast, and the softness of the clouds make my eye immediately disagree with what its seeing.  Unfortunately, that is one of the losses of HDR. Lighting: I like the color layer you have at the bottom of the frame.  I''d really like to see this one processed without the HDR. Composition/Content: Nicely composed, and a perfectly straight horizon.  Very nice. My Opinion: I think that this entry was scored and placed appropriately. Thank you for the opportunity to provide a critique on your entry,Eric ', '2008-12-19 08:55:14.000000', NULL, 0),
(5141097, 745125, 62357, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club<br/> <br/>I think is is an image that did not score as well as it could have done.  I think the composition is very nice, the tree on the left is well placed and gives an anchor point.  The sun is pretty bright so rather difficult to expose correctly.  I am not entirely sure a scene such as this really benefits from the Lucis Arts filter.  I think possibly the effect this filter has had on the sky makes it look a little unreal.  <br/> <br/>I do think a slightly different approach to editing this would have given you a slightly higher score.  I would tried a levels adjustment layer on the dark foreground only, reduced the brightness/exposure very slightly on the sky and then pumped up the vibrancy/saturation a little bit in the blue/cyan/red channels.  I think that would have made a big difference and may have helped the score slightly.<br/> <br/>I do think this image had the potential to score much better.<br/> <br/>Sarah</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club I think is is an image that did not score as well as it could have done.  I think the composition is very nice, the tree on the left is well placed and gives an anchor point.  The sun is pretty bright so rather difficult to expose correctly.  I am not entirely sure a scene such as this really benefits from the Lucis Arts filter.  I think possibly the effect this filter has had on the sky makes it look a little unreal.   I do think a slightly different approach to editing this would have given you a slightly higher score.  I would tried a levels adjustment layer on the dark foreground only, reduced the brightness/exposure very slightly on the sky and then pumped up the vibrancy/saturation a little bit in the blue/cyan/red channels.  I think that would have made a big difference and may have helped the score slightly. I do think this image had the potential to score much better. Sarah', '2008-12-19 14:55:22.000000', NULL, 0),
(5143421, 745489, 54645, '<td valign="top">Hello from the Critique Club. <br/> <br/>I think you did well with the light at hand. I love the color and glow in the background trees. Based on your chosen composition, these particular trees appear to be the subject. Therefore, they should be more in focus, I get a sense that that the focus is a bit off, but this may be a consequence of the snow. I suspect that that this was taken hand held given the aperture of f/4.5 at ISO 100 and shutter of 1/320. A tripod would help tremendously if it was not used. Detail in your chosen subject may be enhanced by a smaller aperture and greater depth of field. I happen to like the composition as you have chosen with the exception of a slight tilt down towards the right. The color in the sky is subtle but effective; I really like the sky in this image.<br/> <br/>With respect to the challenge, I believe you shot yourself in the foot with the title! The title confessed that this was not a sunset or sunrise. Furthermore, IMO, the expectation was for a photograph that directly involved a sunrise or sunset. This definitely affected your score, and is reflected in the in-voting comments. Without the title, it may have finished higher.<br/> <br/>Thank you for submitting your photograph. <br/> <br/>Cheers!<br/> <br/>BS</td>', 'Hello from the Critique Club.  I think you did well with the light at hand. I love the color and glow in the background trees. Based on your chosen composition, these particular trees appear to be the subject. Therefore, they should be more in focus, I get a sense that that the focus is a bit off, but this may be a consequence of the snow. I suspect that that this was taken hand held given the aperture of f/4.5 at ISO 100 and shutter of 1/320. A tripod would help tremendously if it was not used. Detail in your chosen subject may be enhanced by a smaller aperture and greater depth of field. I happen to like the composition as you have chosen with the exception of a slight tilt down towards the right. The color in the sky is subtle but effective; I really like the sky in this image. With respect to the challenge, I believe you shot yourself in the foot with the title! The title confessed that this was not a sunset or sunrise. Furthermore, IMO, the expectation was for a photograph that directly involved a sunrise or sunset. This definitely affected your score, and is reflected in the in-voting comments. Without the title, it may have finished higher. Thank you for submitting your photograph.  Cheers! BS', '2008-12-20 22:53:49.000000', NULL, 0),
(5143562, 744817, 54645, '<td valign="top">Critique Club<br/> <br/>Ironic that your image came up for me. I was conflicted about the processing. I absolutely love the composition despite the horizon bisecting the head of the duck. It is a bit top heavy in that my eye goes directly to the sun then to the duck''s face, and then right back to the sun. Overall I think that works in this image as the sun and face are separated by an interesting middle third with the various water birds.<br/> <br/>Although post processing is part of the art, and is open to interpretation, I believe that you went too far with the noise ninja. The main duck and the ground look fake, and as suggested in a comment below, cartoonish. If it is not via noise ninja, then you left a step out of your details. Maybe it is from curves. Now, on to a more objective critique, there is a very prominent halo around the duck. This is a sign of over processing or less proficient processing. It is suggestive of heavy shadow/highlight manipulation or poor masking, etc. <br/> <br/>I happen to think that you have a spectacular photograph here, and it would have scored much higher with improved or less  heavy handed processing. If you are willing, I would love to see the original photograph. <br/> <br/>Congrats on your high score and finish.</td>', 'Critique Club Ironic that your image came up for me. I was conflicted about the processing. I absolutely love the composition despite the horizon bisecting the head of the duck. It is a bit top heavy in that my eye goes directly to the sun then to the duck''s face, and then right back to the sun. Overall I think that works in this image as the sun and face are separated by an interesting middle third with the various water birds. Although post processing is part of the art, and is open to interpretation, I believe that you went too far with the noise ninja. The main duck and the ground look fake, and as suggested in a comment below, cartoonish. If it is not via noise ninja, then you left a step out of your details. Maybe it is from curves. Now, on to a more objective critique, there is a very prominent halo around the duck. This is a sign of over processing or less proficient processing. It is suggestive of heavy shadow/highlight manipulation or poor masking, etc.  I happen to think that you have a spectacular photograph here, and it would have scored much higher with improved or less  heavy handed processing. If you are willing, I would love to see the original photograph.  Congrats on your high score and finish.', '2008-12-21 02:07:30.000000', NULL, 0),
(5143597, 745485, 67237, '<td valign="top">congrats on the ribbon with this beautiful image!!</td>', 'congrats on the ribbon with this beautiful image!!', '2008-12-21 03:03:11.000000', NULL, 0),
(5144174, 745637, 47852, '<td valign="top"><b><u>Greetings from the Critique Club!</u></b> <br/> <br/><b>First impression</b>: Nice range of color from the blue to the orange, and I like the jagged mountain edges and the depth of the two mountains.  I also like the contrast of the soft clouds and the harsh mountains.  Very simple, and no real focal point, which isn''t a bad thing, but not something many DPC voters will go for.<br/> <br/><b>Further reflections</b>:  Not sure what you could have done to improve this one since it seems like it was shot on a whim and not something you set up intentionally.  Your processing is good, just seems that it didn''t have that ''wow factor'' to garner a higher score from voters.<br/> <br/>I hope you found this critique helpful.  Please feel free to PM me if you have any questions.</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club!  First impression: Nice range of color from the blue to the orange, and I like the jagged mountain edges and the depth of the two mountains.  I also like the contrast of the soft clouds and the harsh mountains.  Very simple, and no real focal point, which isn''t a bad thing, but not something many DPC voters will go for. Further reflections:  Not sure what you could have done to improve this one since it seems like it was shot on a whim and not something you set up intentionally.  Your processing is good, just seems that it didn''t have that ''wow factor'' to garner a higher score from voters. I hope you found this critique helpful.  Please feel free to PM me if you have any questions.', '2008-12-21 14:32:28.000000', NULL, 0),
(5144180, 745353, 47852, '<td valign="top"><b><u>Greetings from the Critique Club!</u></b> <br/> <br/><b>First impression</b>: The sun is clearly the first thing that grabs your attention here, and with the position in the sky it seems to fit the challenge.  The lack of color is surprising for this challenge, but something different is also refreshing.  I like the different tones of the headstones, it helps to set them apart and give depth to the photo.  I don''t know if it''s processing or just the way the photo was shot, but the background trees are jagged, and the foreground has an odd texture about it.  The photo isn''t something I would hang on my wall, but I applaud the uniqueness of it within the context of this challenge.<br/> <br/><b>Further reflections</b>:  After reading your comments, I''m glad that you went in to this with the understanding that it may not score well, but you were willing to take a chance with it anyway as an out of the box type of shot.  It may not have scored high (though it did get over your 5.0!), but it garnered a favorite, and some very favorable comments from voters who appreciated its uniqueness!<br/> <br/><b>To improve</b>:  I realize you didn''t have a lot of flexibility to set up this shot considering where it was taken, but I might have played with the shutter speed a bit, choosing something just a bit faster to keep the sun from being so bright, but while keeping the headstones still nicely exposed.  As far as processing, maybe the funny texture I mentioned above came from the neat image?<br/> <br/>I hope you found this critique helpful.  Please feel free to PM me if you have any questions.</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club!  First impression: The sun is clearly the first thing that grabs your attention here, and with the position in the sky it seems to fit the challenge.  The lack of color is surprising for this challenge, but something different is also refreshing.  I like the different tones of the headstones, it helps to set them apart and give depth to the photo.  I don''t know if it''s processing or just the way the photo was shot, but the background trees are jagged, and the foreground has an odd texture about it.  The photo isn''t something I would hang on my wall, but I applaud the uniqueness of it within the context of this challenge. Further reflections:  After reading your comments, I''m glad that you went in to this with the understanding that it may not score well, but you were willing to take a chance with it anyway as an out of the box type of shot.  It may not have scored high (though it did get over your 5.0!), but it garnered a favorite, and some very favorable comments from voters who appreciated its uniqueness! To improve:  I realize you didn''t have a lot of flexibility to set up this shot considering where it was taken, but I might have played with the shutter speed a bit, choosing something just a bit faster to keep the sun from being so bright, but while keeping the headstones still nicely exposed.  As far as processing, maybe the funny texture I mentioned above came from the neat image? I hope you found this critique helpful.  Please feel free to PM me if you have any questions.', '2008-12-21 14:44:42.000000', NULL, 0),
